---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Elements" prose>
  <h1>Elements</h1>
  <p>
    <code>@hexpunk/core/elements.css</code> ships a token-driven baseline for native HTML
    primitives — paragraphs, links, lists, tables, code, form fields. Import it once at the
    document root and raw HTML in your app picks up the design system's typography, colour,
    focus ring, and forced-colors fallbacks. No <code>&lt;hp-*&gt;</code> wrapper required.
  </p>

  <hp-banner tone="neutral">
    Two layers in one file. The <strong>bare reset</strong> auto-applies to every native
    element. The <strong>.hp-prose</strong> class is an opt-in rhythm scope for long-form
    content. The flowing-text set lives on
    <a href="/components/typography/text">HTML text</a>; the form baseline lives on
    <a href="/components/forms/fields">form fields</a>; tables and details below move
    into Layout as those pages land.
  </hp-banner>

  <h2>Installation</h2>
  <hp-code language="html">{`<!-- Import the three CSS files once at your app's entry. -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/path/to/@hexpunk/core/tokens.dark.css" />
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/path/to/@hexpunk/core/tokens.light.css" />
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/path/to/@hexpunk/core/elements.css" />

<!-- Then write plain HTML — every native element picks up the
     design system's tokens automatically. -->
<p>Hello, hex world.</p>`}</hp-code>

  <p>
    Or, if your build pipeline supports CSS-via-JS imports (Vite, Astro, Next, etc.):
  </p>
  <hp-code language="javascript">{`import "@hexpunk/core/tokens.dark.css";
import "@hexpunk/core/tokens.light.css";
import "@hexpunk/core/elements.css";`}</hp-code>

  <h2>Tables</h2>
  <hp-demo caption="Caption, header, cells">
    <table>
      <caption>Density modes</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Mode</th>
          <th>Cell sm</th>
          <th>Cell md</th>
          <th>Cell lg</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>compact</td><td>44</td><td>80</td><td>140</td></tr>
        <tr><td>default</td><td>50</td><td>100</td><td>180</td></tr>
        <tr><td>comfortable</td><td>60</td><td>120</td><td>220</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <table>
    <caption>Density modes</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Mode</th>
        <th>Cell sm</th>
        <th>Cell md</th>
        <th>Cell lg</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>compact</td><td>44</td><td>80</td><td>140</td></tr>
      <tr><td>default</td><td>50</td><td>100</td><td>180</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</body>`}</hp-code>
  </hp-demo>

  <h2>Details / summary</h2>
  <hp-demo caption="Native disclosure">
    <div>
      <details>
        <summary>Why hexagons?</summary>
        <p>
          Hexagons are the densest regular tiling of the plane — six neighbours per cell,
          uniform distance, and a directionality that maps cleanly to a six-state colour
          system. Every Hexpunk component starts from that geometry.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What's the difference between hp-cell and hp-hex?</summary>
        <p>
          <code>&lt;hp-hex&gt;</code> is the raw geometric primitive — pure shape, no
          semantics. <code>&lt;hp-cell&gt;</code> is the universal labelled atom built
          on top, with interactive variants (anchor / action / secondary / utility) and
          decorative variants (content / support / slot).
        </p>
      </details>
      <details open>
        <summary>Open by default</summary>
        <p>The <code>open</code> attribute starts the disclosure expanded.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
    <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <details>
    <summary>Why hexagons?</summary>
    <p>Hexagons are the densest regular tiling of the plane...</p>
  </details>
  <details open>
    <summary>Open by default</summary>
    <p>The <code>open</code> attribute starts expanded.</p>
  </details>
</body>`}</hp-code>
  </hp-demo>

  <h2>What this file does NOT do</h2>
  <ul class="hp-prose">
    <li>Restyle <code>&lt;hp-*&gt;</code> elements — they live in shadow DOM and own their own chrome.</li>
    <li>Repaint <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> as full hp-button / hp-input lookalikes — hex geometry stays reserved for the components.</li>
    <li>Force a max-width or font-size root override — the page shell is yours.</li>
  </ul>
</Layout>
